  "summary": "The Southern Belle is a stereotypical archetype for a young, upper-class white woman from the American South during the Antebellum period (the decades preceding the American Civil War).",
  "description": "The Southern Belle is a stereotypical archetype for a young, upper-class white woman from the American South during the Antebellum period (the decades preceding the American Civil War). This archetype is defined by a specific code of conduct emphasizing Southern hospitality, charm, and a flirtatious yet chaste demeanor, with the ultimate goal of securing a suitable marriage.\n\nThe associated fashion was designed to highlight the wearer's social status and delicate femininity, characterized by elaborate gowns with corsets, wide hoop skirts, and accessories like gloves and parasols.\n\nThis idealized image was a product of a plantation-based slave economy; the Southern Belle's privileged and leisurely lifestyle was directly dependent on the wealth generated by enslaved labor. This archetype was later heavily romanticized in 20th-century popular culture, most notably in the novel and film Gone with the Wind , which cemented the image in American folklore while often obscuring its foundation in the institution of slavery.",
  "culturalContext": "Key values (per the Aesthetics Wiki): Hospitality, charm, propriety, traditional femininity, family honor, purity",
